+    /**
+     * \brief Records that the display-server connection has been lost
+     *
+     * The adapter is an odd home for a window-system fact, and it is here
+     * for one concrete reason: it is the only object the DXGI and D3D11
+     * shared libraries provably share. DXVK builds libdxvk_dxgi.so and
+     * libdxvk_d3d11.so with only their public entry points exported, so
+     * every static in src/wsi and src/dxvk exists twice, once per library
+     * -- and in a D3D11 process only DXGI's copy ever creates a WsiDriver
+     * [MEASURED: D3D11's `s_driver` is null; the surface is created for it
+     * by DXGI through IDXGIVkSurfaceFactory]. D3D11Device therefore cannot
+     * ask the WSI layer anything at all. It can, however, reach this
+     * object: D3D11CreateDevice takes DXGI's own DxvkAdapter through
+     * IDXGIDXVKAdapter::GetDXVKAdapter (d3d11_main.cpp:34), so both
+     * libraries hold the same instance.
+     *
+     * Losing the connection is process-wide and terminal, which is why an
+     * adapter-wide latch loses no information: every device on it is
+     * equally unable to present.
+     */
+    void markDisplayLost() {
+      m_displayLost.store(true, std::memory_order_release);
+    }
+
+    /**
+     * \brief Whether the display-server connection has been lost
+     * \returns \c true once markDisplayLost has been called
+     */
+    bool isDisplayLost() const {
+      return m_displayLost.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
+    }
